<!-- ===================== SECTION 6: STRATEGIC RECOMMENDATIONS ===================== -->
<div class="section" id="strategic-recommendations">
  <h2>6. Strategic Recommendations</h2>
  <p>The following recommendations are grounded directly in the data findings presented in this report. Each recommendation traces to specific evidence from the analysis.</p>

  <ul class="rec-list">
    <li>
      <strong>1. Launch urgent reskilling programs for Business Analysts and Data Analysts.</strong>
      <p>These two roles represent the most acute displacement risk in the dataset. A phased transition program should move these professionals toward AI-augmented analytics, data science, or product management roles where survival rates are materially higher.</p>
      <div class="evidence">Evidence: Business Analysts face 0.75 avg risk score, 57% at-risk rate, and 0% strong survival. Data Analysts face 0.65 avg risk, 43.5% at-risk rate, and 0% strong survival. No education level mitigates this risk. (Sections 2.3, 5.2, 5.5)</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>2. Invest heavily in AI/ML talent pipelines.</strong>
      <p>AI Researchers and ML Engineers represent the strongest long-term talent investments by every metric: lowest risk, highest survival, highest salaries, and growing demand. Organizations should expand hiring, develop internal talent ladders, and create competitive retention packages for these roles.</p>
      <div class="evidence">Evidence: AI Researchers: 0.15 avg risk, 88.9% strong survival, $42,845 median salary. ML Engineers: 0.25 avg risk, 71.2% strong survival, $39,327 median salary. (Sections 2.3, 3.3, 5.2)</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>3. Prioritize Deep Learning and Python in technical development programs.</strong>
      <p>These two skills are the strongest predictors of job resilience. Upskilling existing workforce in these areas&mdash;particularly reskilling Excel- and SQL-dependent professionals&mdash;offers the highest ROI for displacement mitigation.</p>
      <div class="evidence">Evidence: Deep Learning: 88.9% strong survival, 0% at-risk. Python: 57.9% strong survival, 0.1% at-risk. Excel: 0% strong survival, 57% at-risk. SQL: 0% strong survival, 43.5% at-risk. (Section 4.3)</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>4. Strengthen Cybersecurity and DevOps as AI-resistant workforce pillars.</strong>
      <p>Both roles maintain 0% at-risk rates and above-50% strong survival across the entire time horizon. As AI adoption scales, demand for secure infrastructure and automation operations will grow. These roles should feature prominently in workforce planning.</p>
      <div class="evidence">Evidence: Cybersecurity Analyst: 0.25 avg risk, 53.2% strong survival. DevOps Engineer: 0.30 avg risk, 55.9% strong survival. Both show 0% at-risk rates. (Sections 2.3, 5.2)</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>5. Apply a global rather than regional lens to workforce strategy.</strong>
      <p>AI automation risk is remarkably uniform across all six countries analyzed. Organizations should not assume that certain geographies are insulated from automation. Reskilling and talent strategy should be applied globally with role-specific, not geography-specific, targeting.</p>
      <div class="evidence">Evidence: Country risk scores range only from 0.393 (UK) to 0.401 (India)&mdash;a spread of just 0.008 points. All countries show similar ~0.2-point increases from 2015 to 2035. (Section 2.5)</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>6. Address the pay-risk compounding effect.</strong>
      <p>The negative salary-risk correlation (&minus;0.24) means that displaced workers from high-risk roles will have fewer financial resources to fund their own transitions. Employer-funded reskilling, transition stipends, and bridge compensation should be part of workforce transformation planning, particularly for lower-paid, high-risk roles.</p>
      <div class="evidence">Evidence: High Risk median salary ($21,683) is 37% below Low Risk ($34,105). Business Analysts in India earn a median of ~$7,500&ndash;$9,000 while facing 0.75+ risk scores. (Sections 3.6, 3.2)</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>7. Monitor Software Engineers as an emerging risk category.</strong>
      <p>Software Engineers currently sit in the moderate zone with only 20.5% strong survival and a rising risk trajectory (0.35 in 2015 to 0.55 in 2035). While not yet in the danger zone, this role&mdash;one of the largest in the tech workforce&mdash;warrants active monitoring and proactive upskilling toward AI-augmented development.</p>
      <div class="evidence">Evidence: Software Engineer risk rises from 0.35 to 0.55 (+0.20 over the period), with only 20.5% strong survival and 2.6% at-risk. The Java/Master's combination drops to 18.1% strong survival. (Sections 2.4, 5.2, 5.5)</div>
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Methodology &amp; Data Notes</h2>
  <p><strong>Data Source:</strong> Kaggle AI Job Risk and Salary Dataset (2015&ndash;2035), containing 12,343 synthetically generated records modeled on realistic global job market assumptions. The dataset covers 10 job titles, 6 countries, 3 experience levels, 3 education levels, and 9 primary skills across a 21-year time horizon.</p>
  <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> As a synthetic dataset, results should be interpreted as illustrative trend projections rather than observed market data. Future years (2025&ndash;2035) are modeled projections, not forecasts. Salary values are in USD and do not adjust for purchasing power parity. The near-perfect linear risk trajectories per role reflect the dataset's construction methodology.</p>
  <p><strong>Analysis:</strong> All statistics (means, medians, correlations, distributions) are computed directly from the raw dataset with no imputation or transformation. Survival rates are calculated as the proportion of records with job_survival_class &ge; 1.</p>
</div>

